FINDINGS = [
 ("F1","Background","Chapter 13 of the 3rd edition teaches how a program reaches the web in five steps of growing power: the webbrowser module that opens a page in the user's own browser, the requests module that downloads a page or a file, an API of a weather service answered in JSON, the parsing of HTML with Beautiful Soup, and the control of a real browser with Selenium and with Playwright; it builds three projects on them, showmap.py for OpenStreetMap, searchpypi.py for the search results of PyPI and downloadXkcdComics.py, and closes with 18 practice questions and three practice programs, an image site downloader, the game 2048 and link verification.",["P01"]),
 ("F2","Comparative analysis","The chapter's printed behaviours reproduce wherever they can run offline: the map URL of showmap.py with its address quoted, the status codes and the message of a failed download, the byte counts that write() returns while a response is saved in chunks, the conversion of 285.44 Kelvin to 12.3 Celsius and 54.1 Fahrenheit, and, with requests 2.34.2 and Beautiful Soup 4.15.0 run on the page that the chapter downloads, the matches, texts and attributes the chapter prints for the span with the id author and for the first two paragraphs, and the attribute order alt before src; the download of the page by the book and the ontology of the book agree byte for byte, since the SHA-256 of the page as downloaded equals the digest the book ontology records.",["P01","P03","P07","P13","P15","P29"]),
 ("F3","Comparative analysis","Seven printed things in the chapter cannot be run as printed: the HTML of example3.html as typeset has a typographic quotation mark inside the href of the link, an unescaped p tag in the first paragraph and a misplaced closing form tag, so that on it Beautiful Soup 4.15.0 finds no element with the id author and eight paragraphs, whereas the file the chapter tells the reader to download has the escaped text and the right tags; the chapter says that select('p') gives a list of three matches, but the file has seven paragraphs, three of them inside the form; the file's alt text reads Close up where the chapter prints Close-up; the Step 4 listing of the comic downloader is indented with 8, 12, 15, 12 and then 6 spaces and Python rejects it with an IndentationError; the printed list of sys.argv shows the words St, and Francisco, with a trailing space that shell splitting never produces; and the printed error of raise_for_status names a URL that ends in .html although the requested URL does not, where the message of requests 2.34.2 repeats the requested URL.",["P01","P29","P11","P13","P15"]),
 ("F4","Comparative analysis","Two of the chapter's three projects meet today's web differently from the book: fetching the search page of PyPI with requests on 2026-10-01 returned status 200 with the page titled Client Challenge and no element of the class package-snippet, so searchpypi.py opens nothing, and the robots.txt of pypi.org disallows /search*, a rule that RFC 9309 defines with the wildcard * and that urllib.robotparser in Python 3.14.4 does not apply, since it matches the path as a literal prefix; the XKCD page structure the chapter relies on still held on the same day, with the comic image inside the element with id comic at an address beginning with two slashes and a rel prev link whose href is # on the first comic.",["P01","P22","P05","P28","P13"]),
 ("F5","Comparative analysis","The chapter's advice on requests is correct and incomplete: raise_for_status() after every get() is what the library documents, but the documentation also says that nearly all production code should pass a timeout because a call without one can hang indefinitely (a call with timeout=0.2 raised ReadTimeout, and the same call without one returned the slow server's late answer), and that a download to a file should pass stream=True, because without it the whole body is read before get() returns (with stream=True the first five bytes were read while the server still held the rest), so that iter_content(100000) on a response fetched without stream=True only cuts an already loaded body into chunks.",["P01","P13"]),
 ("F6","Contemporary developments","Current Python replaces or fences several details the chapter uses: PurePath.as_uri() is deprecated since Python 3.14 and removed in 3.19 in favour of Path.as_uri(), which gives the file:// URLs that the chapter suggests for local help pages; datetime.utcfromtimestamp() has been deprecated since 3.12, and datetime.fromtimestamp(), which the chapter names for the dt field of the forecast, answers in the local time zone of the machine, so the same timestamp printed 17:13 with the zone set to Europe/Istanbul and 07:13 with America/Los_Angeles; webbrowser.open() returns True or False and in 3.14 the BROWSER variable can name an already registered browser; and urllib.request.pathname2url has gained add_scheme in 3.14.",["P09","P10","P02","P12","P04"]),
 ("F7","Contemporary developments","The Selenium part of the chapter is one step behind the library: Selenium Manager, shipped with every Selenium release, downloads and finds the driver and the browser by itself, so the manual geckodriver download and the webdriver-manager package that the chapter mentions are no longer needed; the exception the chapter calls NoSuchElement is NoSuchElementException; the text of a WebElement comes from the WebDriver command GET_ELEMENT_TEXT, not from the innerText property that the chapter calls its source; get_attribute looks for a property first and only then for an attribute; and find_element_by_id of older tutorials no longer exists in Selenium 4.50.0.",["P01","P17","P16"]),
 ("F8","Contemporary developments","The Playwright part is accurate against Playwright 1.63.0 and gains from its documentation: locators are strict, so an action on a locator that matches several elements raises, which is why the chapter picks one with nth(0); every action waits for its actionability checks up to a default timeout of 30000 milliseconds, which is the pause the chapter reports for a mistyped selector; a locator has no submit() method, as the chapter says; query_selector() returns None at once when nothing matches; and the library and the browsers are installed in two steps, the package and then the command playwright install, which the chapter writes as python -m playwright install and which Playwright 1.63.0 answers with its usage text.",["P01","P18","P19","P20"]),
 ("F9","Comparative analysis","What a program may fetch is a question the chapter touches only through bandwidth and through the advice to prefer an API: the Robots Exclusion Protocol of RFC 9309 states that its rules are not a form of access authorization, so a robots.txt is a request that a polite crawler honours and the standard library can read it with urllib.robotparser (which gave can_fetch False for a disallowed prefix and exposed Crawl-delay and Request-rate), and an API key identifies an account, so the chapter's advice to keep it in a file and out of the source is the practice an advanced course must add to; the fake key of the chapter's example was answered by OpenWeather with status 401 and a JSON message on 2026-10-01.",["P22","P05","P01","P27"]),
 ("F10","Conclusion","For an advanced course, chapter 13 is best taught as a choice of tool by the page in front of the program: webbrowser when the user should see the page, requests with raise_for_status and a timeout when the URL is known and the answer is text or a file, an API when the service offers one, Beautiful Soup on the text received when the page is static, and Selenium or Playwright only when the page needs a script or a login; the chapter's offline-checkable outputs hold, four printed listings do not run as printed, searchpypi.py meets a challenge page and a robots rule today, and what an advanced course adds is timeouts, streaming, robots.txt, the newer Selenium and Playwright behaviour and the deprecations of current Python.",["P01","P13","P15","P16","P18","P22"]),
]

